WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.680
A few months ago, I had a brilliant&nbsp;
idea: I’d make a tribal gates deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:10.320
with Child of Alara as the commander. I’d run&nbsp;
55 lands along with a bunch of draw and ramp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.300
with Maze’s End, Crackling Perimeter, and a&nbsp;
handful of beatdown options being my wincons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:20.340
A plethora of ways to sacrifice Child of Alara&nbsp;
means that I should have board control mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:25.560
handled, so I only need a mere five slots of&nbsp;
the deck dedicated to interaction of all types.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:31.140
How efficient. Finally, a few options for big&nbsp;
lifegain means that once my land count is up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:35.940
where I want it, I can heal up the damage it took&nbsp;
me to get there. It’s not a high-caliber deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.960
but it certainly scratched my goofy-deck itch,&nbsp;
and it seemed pretty solid for what it was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.300
Unfortunately it was a terrible idea,&nbsp;
chiefly because of the commander.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.360
Now, allow me to lay my credentials on&nbsp;
the table. I am a cruel control player&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:57.660
at heart. My first commander deck was mono black&nbsp;
ruin-your-day with Sheoldred as the commander.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:02.100
My first deck I played with at sanctioned&nbsp;
events was a standard Grixis control deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:07.020
I built just after the release of Born of the&nbsp;
Gods. And my favorite draft I ever played in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:12.900
was a Dragons of Tarkir draft where I picked up&nbsp;
five flattens and seven other removal spells,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:18.240
and spent my night gleefully watching the joy&nbsp;
drain from my opponents’ eyes as creature after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:24.180
creature bit the dust immediately after it was&nbsp;
played. So when I tell you I want a commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:29.940
that just repetitively boardwipes to be a good&nbsp;
idea, I mean it. But it’s not a good idea.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.060
In the context of commander, boardwipes are a&nbsp;
type of effect that I would call “asymmetric”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:42.300
This is confusing on the surface, since they’re&nbsp;
generally literally symmetrical effects—you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:46.980
know, they destroy everything. But what&nbsp;
I mean here is that they impact different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:53.640
players massively differently. Somebody playing a&nbsp;
low-mid power synergistic midrange deck will feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:58.860
them very brutally, but there are a lot of types&nbsp;
of decks that just won’t really care as much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.260
Board wipes won’t stop combos, they won’t&nbsp;
prevent aggro decks from abusing your life total,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:09.540
they won’t slow down large land counts, and they&nbsp;
won’t shut down your opponents generating a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:15.060
of instant and sorcery-based value. The best&nbsp;
option my Child of Alara deck has for dealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:21.120
with somebody suddenly winning is using one of the&nbsp;
two counterspells within the deck, or preparing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.680
to instant-speed sacrifice Child of Alara and&nbsp;
hoping I don’t encounter interaction of any kind.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:32.400
Okay. So, the Child of Alara dependent game plan&nbsp;
has some holes. Big whoop, I’m playing a gates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:39.360
deck man, no shit my deck has weaknesses. However,&nbsp;
this is a bigger problem than it seems. Going back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.860
to the idea of the card being asymmetric, we can&nbsp;
think about how it is experienced by the decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:49.800
that are more vulnerable to it. And the answer&nbsp;
is it’s hell. It sucks so hard for these decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:53.700
they can barely even start to build a board&nbsp;
before they’re staring down the barrel of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:59.460
another Child of Alara sac. Even if they deal with&nbsp;
one of my wipes, as long as my ramp game worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:04.680
out as intended there will just be another one&nbsp;
immediately following it. This is similar to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:10.560
graveyard decks analogy from my last video—this&nbsp;
deck will ruin the day of casual synergy decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.540
but against a less board-dependent deck it’ll&nbsp;
putter around and be a mild nuisance before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:20.580
ultimately getting crushed, with little to no&nbsp;
chance of actually pulling off one of its wincons.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:25.380
Now, Child of Alara is the most extreme&nbsp;
example of board wipes creating highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:28.920
asymmetric game experiences, but this&nbsp;
is an issue with board wipes as a whole.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:35.040
I talked a bit about my Glissa deck in my midrange&nbsp;
video, and that deck operates as a kind of homage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:40.440
to the mono black Sheoldred deck I played when&nbsp;
I was 15. Get card advantage and a ton of mana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:44.580
and proceed to throw bombs and one-sided board&nbsp;
wipes at the table until my opponents fold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:50.700
Now, that deck is much more well-rounded than&nbsp;
the theoretical Child of Alara deck. It has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:55.380
a lot of single target removal to deal with&nbsp;
key threats, and a variety of wincons which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.380 --> 00:04:00.120
can all eliminate a threatening player much&nbsp;
faster than Maze’s End or Crackling Perimeter.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.760
Nonetheless, the profile of how it fares&nbsp;
against different decks looks very similar.&nbsp;

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:11.220
I try not to pull out the deck against the low-mid&nbsp;
power midrange decks that make up a decent portion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:15.660
of decks in my playgroup, because it generally&nbsp;
just stomps on these decks in theatrical fashion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:21.120
However, when my opponents have pressure&nbsp;
to prevent me from fully greeding out or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:26.880
counterspells for my massive mana investments or&nbsp;
combo wins to catch me and my mostly-sorcery-speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:31.080
removal suite off guard, it’s much&nbsp;
worse. Against these sorts of plays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:34.920
my deck ends up looking like one of the&nbsp;
clunky midrange decks that I usually crush.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:40.200
Before going further, I should take the&nbsp;
opportunity to state that board wipes aren’t bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.820
overall. A lot of decks are heavily board-based,&nbsp;
and it’s good to have a solid way to deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:50.040
that. A better version of my Glissa deck would&nbsp;
probably be running 3-5 board wipes instead of 8,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:55.920
with tutors to bridge the gap, but years spent as&nbsp;
a mono-black player forced me into the hard truth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:01.800
that unconditional tutors are bland as hell, so&nbsp;
I don’t play them. Ultimately, the deck is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:06.840
I want it to be, and I’ll happily let it serve&nbsp;
as a teaching point as happily as I’ll play it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:11.580
The real thing I’m trying to convey here is the&nbsp;
need to be mindful when constructing your deck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:15.720
The interaction package of your deck, and what&nbsp;
things it’s particularly good or bad against,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:20.100
is something you should think about.&nbsp;
Ideally, it should be well-balanced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:23.700
and if there are holes they should be&nbsp;
intentional rather than incidental.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:30.480
As an example, my Meria equipments deck has&nbsp;
very little hard removal—burn and noncreature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:35.700
permanent destruction, yes, but not much for&nbsp;
big creatures. This is mostly a matter of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:39.300
fact that I’m playing an aggro deck, and I’m&nbsp;
looking to get a substantial board presence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:44.640
early and be threatening life totals. The deck’s&nbsp;
removal serves to slow down my opponents and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.600
deal with pesky cards like Trigon Predator,&nbsp;
but ultimately the deck’s solution to later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:53.220
threats is to simply deal with the player,&nbsp;
or politic around the threat for a while.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:59.880
My deck’s removal suite has a hole, but it’s&nbsp;
a calculated one. Another common hole in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:03.420
interaction suite would be fast blue decks&nbsp;
that choose to almost entirely run counters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:08.280
that don’t target creatures—a choice motivated by&nbsp;
the fact that most forms of interaction from their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:12.540
opponents are noncreature, and an essence scatter&nbsp;
isn’t gonna help protect a turn 4 combo win.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:19.380
As a final note, let’s return to the idea of&nbsp;
asymmetric effects. A lot of stax effects and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:24.360
hate cards also fall under this label, but stax&nbsp;
effects generally operate the opposite way as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:28.800
board wipes—choking out burstier strategies&nbsp;
that carefully plan out their resources to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.480
execute a specific gameplan, while having less&nbsp;
of an effect on a deck whose gameplan consists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:39.600
of “play treefolk” or something. There’s a reason&nbsp;
why running a load of stax effects is a better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:44.580
idea than running a load of board wipes—the sorts&nbsp;
of decks shut down by an Archon of Emeria are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:49.200
generally stronger and more carefully constructed&nbsp;
than the sort of decks shut down by a Plague Wind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:54.060
You can build a deck with a bunch of stax effects&nbsp;
and a win plan that can outrun a midrange deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:59.100
but you can’t build a deck full of boardwipes&nbsp;
with a win plan that can outrun a combo deck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:03.960
And if you build a deck that massacres casual&nbsp;
midrange decks but flails around against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:07.860
everything else, you’re going to be struggling&nbsp;
to create interesting and dynamic games.

